course: dictionary
lesson: 2175
title: Corrosive: a word with a warning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Corrosive is a word for something that can slowly eat away at things. Read the clues around it. «Warning symbols on bottles show if a product is corrosive, toxic, flammable or harmful.» Bottles and warnings mean danger, so corrosive must mean something harmful.
explain: «The corrosive symbol shows a liquid eating into a hand and a metal bar.» The picture gives the clue: the liquid is eating into things. So corrosive means it can damage or eat away at skin, metal or other materials. Check by swapping in your meaning: "The liquid can eat into skin" still makes sense.
explain: Corrosive can also describe feelings. In «corrosive jealousy», no one is burned, but the jealousy is harmful and slowly damaging, wearing a person down bit by bit, the way acid wears away metal.
explain: Look for words that often sit close to corrosive: substance, symbol, highly corrosive. When you see it on a label, the warning tells you to be careful. A dictionary can confirm the exact meaning if the context is not enough.
fact: «Corrosive» describes something that can slowly damage or eat away metal, skin or other materials.
fact: Strong acids are a common example of a corrosive substance.
fact: The corrosive symbol shows a liquid eating into a hand and a metal bar.
fact: Some cleaning products, such as bleach, oven cleaner and drain cleaner, can burn skin and eyes, so they carry warning symbols.
fact: Rust is a kind of corrosion, which is the slow damage that corrosive things cause.
exercise: «The corrosive symbol shows a liquid eating into a hand and a metal bar.» What does the symbol warn you about? | *Something that can eat into skin and metal | Something that is good to eat | Something that makes a loud noise | Something that is cold to touch
exercise: Some cleaning products can burn skin and eyes. Which word describes them? | Flexible | Gentle | Sweet | *Corrosive
exercise: «Corrosive jealousy» is a phrase. What does corrosive mean in it? | Very cold | Happy and loud | Bright and shiny | *Harmful and slowly damaging
test: Chemicals stay safe in labelled bottles, locked or up high. What do the warning symbols on the label tell you? | What colour the bottle is | How much the bottle weighs | Where to buy the bottle | *How the chemical can hurt you
test: Which word describes a substance that can burn skin and eat away surfaces? | Flammable | Toxic | Harmless | *Corrosive
exam: Mia reads «highly corrosive» on a bottle. What should she do? | Leave the lid open on the table | Drink a little to test it | Shake the bottle hard to mix it | *Read the warning and keep the bottle away from skin and eyes
